<<~ ahu slot=`#/research-github-copilot-s-project-hydrafusion-an`>>

! Research: GitHub Copilot's "Project HydraFusion" (a multi-model, multi-agent workflow controller), and what it means for our swarm

The operator reports that GitHub's Copilot developers announced "Project HydraFusion", a multi-model, multi-agent workflow controller. Study it for two ends: (1) possible integration into our multi-model swarms; (2) inspiration: collide its patterns against our own and name the "pono pattern integrities" (sound, honest structures) worth borrowing, and where ours already hold stronger.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/first-establish-it-exists-at-a-primary-source`>>

!! FIRST: establish it exists, at a primary source
Find the announcement at a primary source (github.blog, docs.github.com, github.com/github or an official GitHub repo, a GitHub staff post or changelog). If you cannot find it at a primary source, SAY SO PLAINLY at the top of your report, list what you did find (secondary coverage, rumours, similarly named projects), and do not describe its features from secondary sources as fact. Never invent details.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/then-with-sources`>>

!! Then, with sources (mark every claim SOURCED with URL and date, or REASONED)
# What it is: status (announced, preview, GA), availability, plan/pricing and how it meters (premium requests? per-agent?), and whether a legacy annual Copilot plan can use it.
# Architecture: how it chooses models per task (routing, fallbacks), the agent roles it defines, orchestration primitives (planner, workers, reviewers, merge or judge steps), state and memory between agents, how agents share or isolate workspaces, human checkpoints, and its integration surfaces (CLI, API, MCP, Actions, IDE).
# Verification and safety: how it reviews or tests agent output, sandboxing and permissions, cost controls and budgets, observability (logs, spend), failure handling.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/our-swarm-to-collide-against`>>

!! Our swarm, to collide against (read; never edit anything)
* Crew law: /home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/mixed-model-swarm.mem (the shapes, the launch, the crew law and its seven refinements) and /home/joshu/.lares-scratch/k17-r/brief.md (#Crew, #Swarm refinements, #Fences).
* The roster research: /home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/ (codex-controller-2026.mem) and /home/joshu/.lares-scratch/research/model-roster/out2.md.
* In short: an Opus controller rules and reads every diff; Sonnet leads drive walled Codex seats (Terra builds, Luna reads, Sol-6.1 on security slices); one writer per worktree; red test before fix as its own commit; one slice per PR; a land gate of CI, held-out cells and a train; spend tallied from each tool's own logs (`spend.py`); a Codex pool floor; Copilot reads the batch Aftermath.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/deliver`>>

!! Deliver
* `out.md` (under 500 lines): the existence finding; what it is; architecture; verification and safety; then COLLISION: a table of patterns (theirs | ours | which holds the stronger integrity, and why), the patterns worth borrowing (each with how it would fit our law), the places ours holds stronger, and INTEGRATION options (adopt as a seat, adopt as the controller of a lane, borrow ideas only, ignore), each with cost, risk and your lean.
* Never read a credential file; never edit the repository.

<<~/ahu>>

